COMMAND NAME: gpexpandsystem

Expands an existing Greenplum Database array by adding new 
segment host resources and then redistributing data across 
the newly expanded array.

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpexpandsystem -h <new_seg_hostfile> [-m <new_mirror_hostfile>] 
[-b] [-k] [-B <parallel_processes>] [-C <rebalance_processes>] 
[-d <master_data_directory>] [-a] [-q] [-l <logfile_directory>] 
[-D]

gpexpandsystem -? | -v


*****************************************************
PREREQUISITES
*****************************************************

* Greenplum Database system is up and running.

* Mirroring is enabled.

* Fault operational mode is read-only (gp_fault_action=readonly).

* All primary and mirror segments are valid (no segment failures 
  reported by gpstate)

* The system is idle with no active connections to the database.

* A recent backup of your databases. The utility will check for 
  backup files in your segment data directories. You can use 
  the -b option to skip this check.

* You are logged in as the Greenplum Database superuser (gpadmin).

* The new segment hosts have been installed and configured as 
  per the existing segment hosts. This involves configuring 
  the hardware and OS, installing the Greenplum software, 
  creating the data directory locations, creating the gpadmin 
  user account, and exchanging SSH keys. See the 
  'Greenplum Database Administrator Guide' for detailed 
  instructions on installing a segment host.

* Enough disk space on your segment hosts to temporarily 
  hold a copy of your largest database table. The utility 
  will verify that you have the required free disk space before 
  it begins the expansion. If you use the -k option, you must 
  have enough free disk space to hold a copy of your entire 
  database.


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpexpandsystem utility adds new segment hosts to an existing 
Greenplum Database array in order to increase data capacity 
and processing performance. For the best performance, Greenplum 
recommends that you do not let your segment host file systems to 
get more than 70 percent full. When disk space on your existing 
segment hosts is nearing this capacity, it is time to add additional 
segment hosts to your system. This utility takes care of adding 
the new segment host resources to the system and rebalancing 
your existing table data across the newly expanded array.

*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-h <new_seg_hostfile>

Required. A file with the name of each new segment host 
you want to add, one name per line, no extra lines or spaces. 
If your segment hosts are multi-homed (multiple network-interface 
host names configured), this file should have all of the configured 
host names of the new segment host(s) you are adding. For example:

     sdw1-1
     sdw1-2
     sdw1-3
     sdw1-4


-m new_mirror_hostfile

Only required if your current mirror segments are configured 
on a different array of hosts than your primary segments. 
This file must have the same number of host names as the 
host file supplied with -h. Each line in this file specifies 
the mirror host name and data directory location of a 
mirror segment in the format of: 

      mirror_seg_hostname:mirror_data_directory_location


-r (do not rebalance tables with random distribution)

Do not rebalance the data across the newly expanded array 
for tables that were created with a random distribution 
policy (DISTRIBUTED RANDOMLY). The default is to rebalance 
the data for randomly distributed tables.


-b (continue without backup files)

Greenplum strongly recommends that you have a recent 
backup of all databases before doing an expansion. The 
utility will check for backup files in your segment 
data directories. If you choose not to do a backup, 
or if you have secured your backup files in an alternate 
location, use this option to skip this check.


-k (keep temporary expansion schemas)

In order to rebalance the data across the newly added 
resources, the utility creates a temporary copy of each 
database schema. Use this option to keep the temporary 
schemas in the database. The default is to drop this 
temporary schema after the expansion and data rebalancing 
is complete.


-B <parallel_processes>

The number of segment host verification processes to start 
in parallel. If not specified, the utility will start up to 
4 parallel processes depending on how many segment host 
verification tests it needs to perform.


-C <rebalance_processes>

The number of table data rebalancing processes to start in 
parallel. If not specified, the utility will start up to 4 
parallel processes depending on how many tables it needs 
to redistribute.


-d <master_data_directory>

Optional. The master host data directory. If not specified, 
the value set for $MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.


-a (do not prompt)

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.


-q (no screen output)

Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the screen,
 but is still written to the log file.

-l <logfile_directory>

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets log output level to debug.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (show utility version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and checksum 
of this utility.

*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Add new segment hosts (as listed in new_seghost_file) to an 
existing Greenplum Database system:

gpexpandsystem -h new_seghost_file


Add new segment hosts (as listed in new_seghost_file) and 
mirror hosts (as listed in new_mirror_hostfile) to an existing 
Greenplum Database system that has mirror segments configured 
on a different array of hosts than the primaries:

gpexpandsystem -h new_seghost_file -m new_mirror_hostfile


Add new segment hosts to an existing Greenplum Database 
system and skip the check for backup files:

gpexpandsystem -h new_seghost_file -b

